t is smaller than the full candidate list shown.\n\nActually, the material shows 120 candidates (workingSetCount 120). But perhaps only a portion is \"exposed\" at a time, and expand reveals more. Since expand returned exhausted with remainingCount 0, all candidates should be exposed now.\n\nWait, but the error happened before I expanded. So at the time of my publish, item-167782ebe824 wasn't exposed. After expanding, it should be exposed now. Let me retry the publish.\n\nActually, the expand returned exhausted with no candidates, meaning there was nothing more to expose - the working set was already fully exposed. But then why was item-167782ebe824 not exposed?\n\nHmm. Let me reconsider. Perhaps the candidates list in the material is the full eligible set (120), but the \"exposed\" working set is a deterministic segment that's smaller. The expand function exposes the next segment. Since it returned exhausted, maybe the full set is now exposed.\n\nLet me retry the publish now that I've called expand. Perhaps item-167782ebe824 is now exposed.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. The expand returned \"exhausted\" with remainingCount 0 and no candidates. This suggests there was nothing to expand - meaning the working set was already fully exposed. But then item-167782ebe824 should have been exposed.\n\nLet me reconsider - maybe the candidates shown in the material ARE the exposed working set, and item-167782ebe824 is genuinely among them. But the publish failed saying it's not exposed. \n\nThis is contradictory. Let me re-examine. Perhaps the issue is that the material's candidate list is the full eligible set, but the actual \"exposed\" set at publish time was a subset, and my expand call now exposed the rest. Since expand returned exhausted (nothing more), perhaps now everything is exposed.\n\nLet me just retry the publish with the same selection.","type":"text"},{"arguments":{"items":[{"candidateId":"evaluation:candidate:item-07ad7c7c66a5","recommendationReason":"讲